    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1017">

                <pb facs="1061" n="775" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg775"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72111/4127</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Denmark</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Egan</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Copenhagen</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-10-14T13:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 14, 1916, 1 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 11.50 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>368. Your circular, October 10, 1 p. m.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d1017-f1"/> Danish
                    Government have issued no decrees or decisions concerning war submarines except
                    as contained in its law of December 20, 1912, the substance of which is that war
                    submarines shall be treated as other war vessels. The law follows closely the,
                    principles of the convention of The Hague. The question of merchant submarines
                    has not arisen and the Government is not willing to give a decision until that
                    question shall arise.</p>
                <p>This statement is practically the same as contained in the reply of the Danish
                    Government dated October 6 to the Allies’ collective note of August 27. 1 am
                    forwarding by mail a copy of note which, at the request of the Danish
                    Government, is to be treated as strictly confidential.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Egan</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
